The quiz block is now a fixed size. Across a full 10-question run plus back-and-forth navigation, the `.quiz` section stays at 605px, the page's scroll height stays at 6181px, the question card's top edge stays at 5707px, and the feedback slot stays at 82px — every one of those was identical at all 22 sample points.

Rather than reserve a hardcoded two lines, the script measures the tallest question card and the tallest feedback panel for the actual quiz content at the current width, then pins those as `min-height`:

```221:240:skills/interactive-explainer-html/references/explainer-template.html
      /* Reserve the tallest question card and feedback panel so the quiz block never resizes. */
      function reserveQuizSpace() {
        if (displayedQuestions.length === 0) return;
        let cardHeight = 0;
        let panelHeight = 0;
        displayedQuestions.forEach((question, index) => {
          cardHeight = Math.max(cardHeight, measureHiddenHeight(quizRoot, buildQuestionCard(question, index)));
```

That way a three-line explanation is never clipped and a wrapped prompt never pushes anything around. It re-measures after `document.fonts.ready` (the embedded font loads after first paint, which would otherwise leave the reservation slightly short) and on window resize, since wrapping changes with width. There's still a CSS floor of `calc(2lh + 1.7rem)` on the slot so the space is reserved before any JavaScript runs.

Fixing the size this way let me delete most of the animation machinery. The grid `0fr`/`1fr` collapse trick, the animated `margin`, the measured height transitions on both the stack and the question region, and the separate in-flow versus absolute exit paths are all gone. Every feedback panel is now absolutely stacked in the slot, so showing, hiding, and swapping feedback are all the same operation: fade the old one out, fade the new one in. `transitionFeedbackForQuestion` went from roughly 55 lines and four branches down to 20 lines and one.

One thing I changed on my own judgment, worth a look: because panels are now created and destroyed instead of having their text rewritten, a `role="status"` on them would have stopped announcing to screen readers (a live region has to be in the DOM *before* its content changes). So `#quiz-feedback` is now a permanent visually-hidden status element that holds the announcement text, and the visual panels are `aria-hidden="true"`. The answer buttons' `aria-describedby` still points at it, and the artifact validator's ARIA wiring checks still pass.

The empty slot renders as blank space rather than an empty bordered box — that read to me as what you meant by the space being "accounted for," but if you'd rather see a faint outline sitting there waiting for the answer, that's a one-line change.

Here's the same question before and after answering, showing the reserved space being filled without anything else moving:

